# Runtime environment source pins (SR-21). Hand-written; `requirements.lock` is
# generated from this file and is what actually gets installed.
#
#   uv pip compile requirements.in --generate-hashes -o requirements.lock \
#       --index-strategy unsafe-best-match
#   uv pip sync requirements.lock
#   .venv/bin/python -c "import torch; assert torch.version.cuda is not None, 'CPU BUILD'"
#
# Every version here is read off `spec/params.generated.yaml` under `environment:`,
# which is the single source of truth (SR-1). Do not edit a pin here without
# amending the spec first.
#
# THE TRAP (AM-23): torch MUST come from the cu130 index below. A bare resolve
# against PyPI silently yields the CPU build, and the only check that catches it
# is `torch.version.cuda is not None` -- not a successful import. That check is
# `tests/test_env.py::test_cuda_build`, which hard-asserts with no escape hatch.
#
# `--index-strategy unsafe-best-match` is needed on the compile command because
# uv's default `first-index` stops at the first index carrying the package NAME,
# and PyPI carries `torch` but not the `+cu130` local version.

--extra-index-url https://download.pytorch.org/whl/cu130

# --- runtime stack (params.environment) -------------------------------------
torch==2.13.0+cu130
torchvision==0.28.0+cu130
numpy==2.5.1
pillow==12.3.0
scikit-image==0.26.0
glymur==0.14.3
pytest==9.1.1

# --- spec tooling, deliberately inside the lock (AM-65) ----------------------
# `uv pip sync` makes the environment EXACTLY the lockfile: anything absent is
# uninstalled. The spec tooling (`tools/gen_spec_views.py`,
# `tools/check_doc_consistency.py`) needs PyYAML and lives in the same venv, so
# omitting it here would have the first command of W1 uninstall it and break the
# three checks that guard the spec. `requirements.txt` stays as the
# dependency-light bootstrap for a machine with nothing else installed.
PyYAML==6.0.3

# NOT here on purpose:
#   sionna-no-rt==2.0.1  -- W3, when the LDPC chain is integrated (DEC-10).
#
# OpenJPEG 2.5.4 is also not repository-provisioned: it is a system library,
# externally installed and exactly verified before any J2K path (AM-75).
# `glymur` above is only its pinned Python binding.
